## Version
* Phaser Version: 4.2.1 (master @ 02d8931)
* Operating system: macOS 27.0.1, Apple M1 Pro
* Browser: Chrome 156

## Description

Arcade Physics rebuilds the dynamic `RTree` every step and searches it for every body in a group collider. In both paths, each leaf item is converted to a bounding box via `toBBox`, which allocates a new object per item visited:

- `RTree#search` / `#collides`: `childBBox = node.leaf ? toBBox(child) : child` (`src/structs/RTree.js:82,123`)
- `distBBox` (bulk load): `extend(destNode, node.leaf ? toBBox(child) : child)` (`src/structs/RTree.js:656`)
- `toBBox` returns `{ minX: a.left, minY: a.top, maxX: a.right, maxY: a.bottom }` (`src/structs/RTree.js:618`)

A leaf holds up to 16 items, so every search allocates up to 16 objects per leaf it touches. With 300 bodies colliding against their own group that's about 500 KB of garbage per frame.

## Example Test Code

```js
class Swarm extends Phaser.Scene {
    create() {
        const tex = this.textures.createCanvas('e', 24, 24);
        tex.getContext().fillRect(0, 0, 24, 24);
        tex.refresh();
        const g = this.physics.add.group({ bounceX: 1, bounceY: 1, collideWorldBounds: true });
        for (let i = 0; i < 300; i++) g.create(Math.random() * 1240 + 20, Math.random() * 680 + 20, 'e').setVelocity(Math.random() * 200 - 100, Math.random() * 200 - 100);
        this.physics.add.collider(g, g);
    }
}
new Phaser.Game({ type: Phaser.WEBGL, width: 1280, height: 720, physics: { default: 'arcade' }, scene: Swarm });
```

## Additional Information

The RTree's format is fixed to `left/top/right/bottom` (see `compareMinX`/`compareMinY`), so leaf items can be tested and extended directly from those properties without allocating. Whole-frame step time, median of 600 frames × 3 runs:

| Bodies | master | patched | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| 100 | 0.09–0.10 ms | 0.08 ms | about −15% |
| 300 | 0.29–0.32 ms | 0.255–0.265 ms | −13% |
| 600 | 0.705–0.715 ms | 0.60–0.615 ms | −14% |

Allocations at 300 bodies: 503 KB/frame → 167 KB/frame. Search results are identical. PR to follow.

## Version
* Phaser Version: 4.2.1 (master @ 02d8931)
* Operating system: macOS 27.0.1, Apple M1 Pro
* Browser: Chrome 156

## Description

`TilemapLayerWebGLRenderer` sends every visible tile through the Submitter/Transformer nodes, and those nodes recompute values that are constant for the whole layer on every tile:

- `TransformerTile#run` copies the camera view matrix with scroll factor and multiplies the parent matrix (`src/renderer/webgl/renderNodes/transformer/TransformerTile.js:72-81`).
- `TransformerTile#run` builds a full ITRS matrix and multiplies it, even when the tile isn't rotated, which is almost always (`:120-129`).
- `SubmitterTile#run` calls `setRenderOptions`, which reads the texture, lighting and game config for every tile (`src/renderer/webgl/renderNodes/submitter/SubmitterTile.js:132`).

A 1280x720 view with 32px tiles draws over 900 tiles per layer per frame, so this per-tile overhead is most of the layer's CPU cost.

## Example Test Code

```js
class Tiles extends Phaser.Scene {
    create() {
        const tex = this.textures.createCanvas('tiles', 512, 32);
        for (let i = 0; i < 16; i++) { tex.getContext().fillStyle = `hsl(${i * 22},80%,50%)`; tex.getContext().fillRect(i * 32 + 2, 2, 28, 28); }
        tex.refresh();
        for (const fill of [1, 0.3, 0.1]) {
            const data = [...Array(80)].map(() => [...Array(120)].map(() => Math.random() < fill ? (Math.random() * 16) | 0 : -1));
            const map = this.make.tilemap({ data, tileWidth: 32, tileHeight: 32 });
            map.createLayer(0, map.addTilesetImage('tiles'), 0, 0);
        }
        this.cameras.main.setBounds(0, 0, 120 * 32, 80 * 32);
        this.n = 0;
    }
    update() { this.n++; this.cameras.main.setScroll(this.n * 3 % 2400, this.n * 2 % 1800); }
}
```

## Additional Information

Computing the layer matrix and render options once per layer, and translating/scaling directly when `tile.rotation === 0`, takes the step time of the scene above from 0.235–0.245 ms to 0.19 ms (−20%), median of 600 frames × 3 runs. Output is pixel-identical, including rotated/flipped/tinted tiles, scroll factor, scaled layers and rotated/zoomed multi-camera views. PR to follow.

## Version
* Phaser Version: 4.2.1 (master @ 02d8931)
* Operating system: macOS 27.0.1, Apple M1 Pro
* Browser: Chrome 156 (WebGL on ANGLE/Metal)

## Description

Scenes that break the batch often (Shapes, Graphics, Text and Images interleaved, typical for menus, HUDs and card games) lose a lot of frame rate in Chrome. The time is spent in the GPU process, so it doesn't show up in a JS CPU profile. This is likely what was reported in #7171 (UI with rectangles lagging in Chrome but not Firefox).

Every batch flush uploads its vertices with `WebGLBufferWrapper#update(bytes)`, i.e. a partial `bufferSubData` into the same large buffer (16384 quads, ~1.8 MB for `BatchHandlerQuad`), which an earlier draw call in the same frame still references:

- `src/renderer/webgl/renderNodes/BatchHandlerQuad.js:612`
- `src/renderer/webgl/renderNodes/BatchHandlerTriFlat.js:330,336`
- `src/renderer/webgl/wrappers/WebGLBufferWrapper.js:221`

To keep the earlier draw's data intact, the driver has to copy or stall on the whole buffer for every partial update. With 96 UI buttons (rectangle + icon + label) that's 144 uploads and 96 draw calls per frame.

Measured in a real `requestAnimationFrame` loop, headless Chrome 156, average FPS over 300 frames, 3 runs each:

| Buttons | Draw calls / frame | FPS (master) | FPS (respecified upload) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 24 | 48 | 60 | 60 |
| 48 | 96 | 48–54 | 60 |
| 96 | 192 | 28–32 | 60 |
| 192 | 384 | 15 | 59–60 |

## Example Test Code

```js
class Buttons extends Phaser.Scene {
    create() {
        const tex = this.textures.createCanvas('icon', 32, 32);
        tex.getContext().fillRect(0, 0, 32, 32);
        tex.refresh();
        for (let i = 0; i < 96; i++) {
            const x = 20 + (i % 6) * 210, y = 20 + (Math.floor(i / 6) * 85) % 680;
            this.add.rectangle(x, y, 200, 70, 0x223344).setOrigin(0).setStrokeStyle(2, 0xffffff);
            this.add.image(x + 30, y + 35, 'icon');
            this.add.text(x + 60, y + 25, 'Item ' + i, { fontSize: '18px' });
        }
    }
}
new Phaser.Game({ type: Phaser.WEBGL, width: 1280, height: 720, scene: Buttons });
```

Watch the FPS meter in Chrome (or `game.loop.actualFps`).

## Additional Information

Uploading dynamic buffers with `bufferData(target, view.subarray(0, bytes), usage)` instead re-specifies the store, so the driver can hand out fresh memory instead of preserving the old contents. It's the same number of GL calls. With this change all of the above runs at 60 fps, and every other scene I tried was unchanged (single-batch sprites, tilemaps, particles, tweens, GPU layers, filters). PR to follow.

## Version
* Phaser Version: 4.2.1 (master @ 02d8931)
* Operating system: macOS 27.0.1
* Browser: Chrome 156 (WebGL renderer)

## Description

In WebGL, tiles with `flipY = true` render offset by one tile up and to the right of where they belong. The Canvas renderer draws them correctly.

`TransformerTile#run` adds the vertical flip offset to `x` instead of `y`:

```js
if (element.flipY)
{
    height *= -1;
    x += frameHeight;   // should be y
}
```

`src/renderer/webgl/renderNodes/transformer/TransformerTile.js:117`. The unit test currently encodes the wrong quad (`y + height = -48`).

## Example Test Code

```js
class FlipY extends Phaser.Scene {
    create() {
        const tex = this.textures.createCanvas('tiles', 32, 32);
        tex.getContext().fillStyle = '#f00';
        tex.getContext().fillRect(2, 2, 28, 28);
        tex.refresh();
        const map = this.make.tilemap({ data: [[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]], tileWidth: 32, tileHeight: 32 });
        const layer = map.createLayer(0, map.addTilesetImage('tiles'), 64, 64);
        layer.forEachTile(t => { t.flipY = true; });
    }
}
new Phaser.Game({ type: Phaser.WEBGL, width: 320, height: 240, scene: FlipY });
```

The tiles are symmetric, so the flipped layer should look identical to an unflipped one. On master it's shifted by 32px both up and right.

## Additional Information

With symmetric tiles, rendering a flipped layer and diffing it against the unflipped one: master differs by 89,376 pixels for `flipY` and `flipX+flipY`, the fix differs by 0 for `flipX`, `flipY` and both. PR to follow.

