While benchmarking current master (4efbec1) I ran the default newlines syntax set over the syntax tests in `testdata/Packages`, and three of them panic: `Java/tests/syntax_test_java.java`, `Java/tests/syntax_test_java_indentation.java` and `Markdown/tests/syntax_test_markdown.md`. I shrank them down to a few lines each:

```rust
use syntect::easy::HighlightLines;
use syntect::highlighting::ThemeSet;
use syntect::parsing::SyntaxSet;
use syntect::util::LinesWithEndings;

fn main() {
    let ss = SyntaxSet::load_defaults_newlines();
    let ts = ThemeSet::load_defaults();
    let inputs = [
        ("java", "@MultiLineAnnotation(\n  foo = BAR,\n)\nclass X {}\n"),
        ("java", "if (x)\n  return\n}\nif (v.isNull() == TRUE)\n"),
        ("md", "  $$\n\n"),
    ];
    for (ext, src) in inputs {
        let syntax = ss.find_syntax_by_extension(ext).unwrap();
        let mut h = HighlightLines::new(syntax, &ts.themes["base16-ocean.dark"]);
        for line in LinesWithEndings::from(src) {
            h.highlight_line(line, &ss).unwrap();
        }
    }
}
```

Each one panics on its own:

1. Java annotation: `src/highlighting/highlighter.rs:159:30: byte index 20 is out of bounds of "  foo = BAR,\n"`
2. Java `if`: `src/parsing/parser.rs:1279:40: byte index 14 is out of bounds of "        }\n"` (that's with the original indentation, same spot with the short version)
3. Markdown `$$`: `src/parsing/parser.rs:1307:39: byte index 2 is out of bounds of "\n"`. A bare `$$\n\n` also panics, but in `highlighter.rs:159` instead.

All three go through the cross-line `fail` handling in `handle_fail`. It looks like `match_start_pos`, which is a byte offset on the line where the branch was taken, gets applied to a different line:

- In case 1 the ops returned for line 2 contain a `Push(meta.group.java)` at byte 20, which is where the `(` is on line 1. Line 2 is only 13 bytes long, and `RangedHighlightIterator` slices out of bounds.
- In case 2 it's `replay_line[match_start_pos..]` on the first replayed line (`parser.rs:1279`).
- In case 3 it's `line[match_start_pos..]` in the same-line exhaustion branch (`parser.rs:1307`), even though the branch point came from the previous line.

As far as I can tell this came in with the deferred replay work (d7fded6, b090f98), so v5.3.0 isn't affected. These are all half-typed, invalid snippets, but that's normal input for an editor highlighting while you type.

For comparison, `Perl/syntax_test_perl.pl` doesn't panic, but it never seems to finish with the default dump (I gave up after 8 minutes). Might be a separate thing.

Keyword tables are checked with `in` (`index.js:75,78,83`), which also matches `Object.prototype` members. Two visible effects:

```js
const traverse = require('json-schema-traverse')

// 1. inherited names treated as keywords
traverse({toString: {foo: {type: 'string'}}}, {cb: (s, p) => console.log(p)})
// '' and '/toString/foo'  (toString handled as a propsKeyword)

traverse({valueOf: {type: 'number'}}, {allKeys: true, cb: (s, p) => console.log(p)})
// ''  ('/valueOf' is never visited, its value is iterated as a props map instead)

// 2. keyword not escaped in allKeys pointers (index.js:84)
traverse({'a/b': {}}, {allKeys: true, cb: (s, p) => console.log(p)})
// '/a/b', expected '/a~1b'
```

ajv uses these pointers to resolve local `$id`s / refs, so an unescaped key produces a wrong pointer.

Proposed fix: `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(table, key)` for the four lookups (tables stay public and mutable), and `escapeJsonPtr(key)` in the keyword branch.

